WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:02.640
It is very early in the morning

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.970
and the air is very cold
here at Kerosene Creek,

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.520
a natural hot spring near Rotorua
on the North Island of New Zealand.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.020
It's a natural swimming pool and waterfall

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.880
at a warm and comfortable
bathing temperature year-round

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.780
because of the geothermal
activity that's around here.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.190
There is also a questionable
smell in the air,

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:23.060
but fortunately that doesn't
really come across on camera.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.290
This place is not the local
secret that it used to be.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.630
Tourists flock here now,
which is why I'm filming here so early.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.800
I didn't really want to turn up
to a swimming pool full of people

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.600
and awkwardly
point a camera at them.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:37.530
But there's one view that I can show you
that the tourists won't get to see

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:38.490
because there's a rule here

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.410
that the government warnings
are very clear about:

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.260
keep your head above water.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.220
- The brain-eating amoeba can be found
in hot pools and things like that.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.340
So these organisms enter the nose

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.600
if you put your head under
or you snort the water in.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.750
They can then go from the nose
through the olfactory nerves to the brain.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:02.100
And then once in the brain they start to
eat the neurons and astrocytes.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.080
And they cause massive inflammation.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.910
And then you get this
horrible amoeboid disease.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:12.300
It starts sometimes with a change in
your sense of smell, sense of taste.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.840
And then that can progress to headaches,

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:19.900
vomiting, fever, and then
ultimately to seizures and death.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.100
There are potentially a
couple of drugs that are used,

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.320
one of which is not licenced for use,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.000
and is actually very
difficult to get hold of.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.030
So in general there is no treatment,

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:31.390
and 97% of people die.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.250
- Brain-eating amoeba
aren't unique to here

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:34.460
or even to New Zealand.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.640
They're common anywhere that
there's warm, untreated water.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.000
There was even a fatal case
at Disney World in Florida in 1980,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.650
back when they had a
water park built into a lake.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.950
And there are more odd cases too.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.790
- When you've got a cold
and you're really bunged up

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:51.360
in your sinuses, you can flush them out.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.300
It's really important, if you're going to
shove water up your nose,

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.640
because of the fact that
there is this amazing--

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.100
these many nerves connecting
your nose to your brain,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.650
that you use only boiled water.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.110
And so there have been cases of people

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:07.330
who've used neti pots
just taking tap water.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:10.360
And, ugh! They...

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.240
Brain-eating amoeba is not
the only infection people have had.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:14.400
There are other things,

00:02:14.403 --> 00:02:16.600
but that is one potential thing
people can get.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.150
- But despite the terrifying name,
the grisly stories,

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.580
and the 90-something percent
chance of death if you're infected,

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:24.960
infection is really rare.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:27.490
How much of that is down to
the government warnings though?

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:30.790
Well, probably best not to have
a control group for that experiment.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:35.440
Although, that said, the warning signs
that were once here aren't here any more.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:36.800
- So what are the odds of it happening?

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:38.750
They're very, very, very rare.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.790
The last case we had in New Zealand
was, I think, in 2000.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:45.610
But I guess the point is when it
does happen, it's pretty catastrophic.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:47.470
There will be things that
we're doing in our daily lives

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:50.210
that are way more dangerous than this,

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:52.460
even from a microbial point of view.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:54.990
We are covered in microbes,

00:02:54.990 --> 00:03:00.640
we have really nasty microbes living
in us and on us on a daily basis,

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.750
and most of the time it's okay.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:05.120
Maybe one in three, one in four people

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.590
will have staph aureus
living up their nose.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:09.280
Pretty fine up there, but if you get that

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.010
in your bloodstream you can
end up with horrible infection.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:14.490
You're more at risk, frankly,
from shoving your finger

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.180
up your nose and then
touching a cut accidentally

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:21.460
than you are from having
your brain eaten by amoeba.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:23.570
- Thanks to Dr. Wiles at
the University of Auckland

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.550
for all her expertise.
Pull down the description

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:27.750
for the references she
used in her research.

